        "faqs": [
            {
                "question": "What does DIN EN 10204 3.1 mean and why does Germany require it?",
                "answer": "EN 10204 Type 3.1 (Inspection Certificate) means a lot-specific material certificate issued by the manufacturer's own authorized inspection department (not by the production department), containing chemical composition + mechanical test results for the specific heat/lot delivered. Germany requires EN 10204 3.1 because the German supply chain quality system (BMW SQA, VW FORMEL Q, Mercedes-Benz SQBP, PED 2014/68/EU) mandates traceable material testing \u2014 not just a generic declaration of compliance. EN 10204 3.2 is required when an independent body (T\u00dcV Rheinland) must witness the testing."
            },
            {
                "question": "What is the difference between T\u00dcV Rheinland NB 0035 and T\u00dcV S\u00dcD for German PED forgings?",
                "answer": "Both T\u00dcV Rheinland (NB 0035, Cologne) and T\u00dcV S\u00dcD (NB 0123, Munich) are EU Notified Bodies for PED 2014/68/EU. T\u00dcV Rheinland is preferred by clients in NRW (Thyssenkrupp, Bayer, BASF, Henkel), Hessen (BASF Frankfurt, Celanese), and Northern Germany (Hamburg/Bremen industrial clients). T\u00dcV S\u00dcD is preferred by Bavarian clients (BMW, MAN, Linde, MTU). We work with T\u00dcV Rheinland as our primary NB (NB 0035 CE marking certificate) but can arrange T\u00dcV S\u00dcD witness testing for Bavarian clients on request."
            },
            {
                "question": "What DIN EN 10083-3 grades are most commonly specified by German automotive OEMs?",
                "answer": "The three most commonly specified DIN EN 10083-3 grades in German automotive forging procurement: (1) 42CrMo4 (1.7225 \u2014 Rm \u22651,000 MPa/25mm diameter) for crankshafts (BMW B47, Mercedes OM654d, VW EA888, DEUTZ TCD 6.1), gearbox forks, and suspension arms; (2) 18CrNiMo7-6 (1.6587 \u2014 case-hardening, carburized to HRC 58\u201362 surface) for gearbox planetary carriers (ZF 8HP), synchronizer hubs, and sun gear shafts; (3) 34CrNiMo6 (1.6582 \u2014 Rm \u22651,150 MPa/25mm) for high-torque M-class engine crankshafts (BMW S58, Porsche GT3 RS, Mercedes AMG M177 V8)."
            },
            {
                "question": "Can you supply DIN EN 10025-6 S690Q high-strength structural forgings for offshore wind?",
                "answer": "Yes. For offshore wind applications (E.ON, RWE, \u00d8rsted German offshore projects): monopile transition piece structural forgings in S460MLO to EN 10225 (specific offshore steel standard \u2014 Charpy \u226542 J at \u221240\u00b0C longitudinal, Z35 through-thickness), or S460Q/S690Q to DIN EN 10025-6 (for land-based offshore wind foundations). DNV GL certification (GL Noble Denton for offshore structural steel survey). EN 10204 3.2 (T\u00dcV Rheinland or Bureau Veritas witnessed)."
            },
            {
                "question": "What is the Lieferkettensorgfaltspflichtengesetz (LkSG) requirement for Indian forge shops?",
                "answer": "German companies with \u22651,000 employees (effective 2024) must conduct supply chain human rights and environmental due diligence. For Indian forge shops: (1) ILO compliance \u2014 no child labor, minimum wage compliance under Minimum Wages Act, Factories Act safety compliance; (2) Environmental \u2014 Gujarat SPCB consent-to-operate, ISO 14001 certification; (3) Ethics \u2014 no corruption per anti-bribery law; (4) Documentation \u2014 annual LkSG supplier declaration. JK Forge provides a comprehensive LkSG compliance package to all German customers, reviewed by our legal counsel in Frankfurt (Hasche Sigle)."
            },
            {
                "question": "Is ASME SA-grade material accepted in Germany as equivalent to DIN EN grades?",
                "answer": "Yes, with documentation. ASME SA182 F316L = EN 10222-5 / EN 10272 Grade X2CrNiMo17-12-2 (1.4404 equivalent); ASME SA182 F22 = EN 10222-2 Grade 10CrMo9-10 (1.7380 equivalent). German PED-certified vessels must specify EN material grades (or reference ASME equivalents in the PED conformity documentation). We provide equivalence tables (DIN-ASME comparison) with every material certificate, enabling German engineers to confirm PED compliance without rework of their vessel design calculations."
            }
        ]
